blew — vendored, modified copy
==============================

This directory contains a vendored copy of the Rust half of `blew`, a
cross-platform async Bluetooth Low Energy library (Apple, Linux, Android).

Upstream project : https://github.com/mcginty/blew
Upstream version : 0.5.1-beta.8 (git tag `blew-v0.5.1-beta.8`, crates/blew);
                   first vendored at 0.3.0, rebased onto 0.5.1-beta.8 on 2026-11-02
Original author  : Jake McGinty <me@jakebot.org>
License          : GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 or later
                   (AGPL-3.0-or-later) — see the `LICENSE` file in this
                   directory for the full license text.

This modified copy remains licensed under AGPL-3.0-or-later, the same license
as the upstream project.

The Android Kotlin half of blew was vendored separately, in July 2026, into
modules/iroh-location/android/src/main/java/org/jakebot/blew/ (notice in
modules/iroh-location/android/third_party/blew/). It is not duplicated here,
and upstream's `android/` and `examples/` directories and its JNI parity test are
omitted.


Modifications by the streetCryptid project
------------------------------------------

In accordance with section 5 of the GNU AGPL, the changes made to the upstream
source after it was vendored into this repository are dated and described
below. Entries before 2026-11-02 describe changes to 0.3.0; the 2026-11-02
entry says which of them survive on top of 0.5.1-beta.8.

2026-10-31 — Apple flow control (CoreBluetooth backend):

  * Cargo.toml
      - Added this vendoring header, `publish = false`, and removed the
        `include` list. Added `objc2-core-foundation` (Apple targets) for the
        L2CAP reactor's run-loop source.

  * build.rs
      - `android_dir` now points at the module's vendored Kotlin sources.

  * src/platform/apple/peripheral.rs
      - `notify_characteristic` no longer drops a notification when
        CoreBluetooth's transmit queue is full. Upstream ignored the return
        value of `updateValue:forCharacteristic:onSubscribedCentrals:`; it now
        waits for `peripheralManagerIsReadyToUpdateSubscribers:` (new delegate
        method) and sends the value again, for at most 2 s.

  * src/platform/apple/central.rs
      - A write without response is sent only when `canSendWriteWithoutResponse`
        is true; otherwise it waits for
        `peripheralIsReadyToSendWriteWithoutResponse:` (new delegate method),
        for at most 2 s. Upstream wrote unconditionally, which CoreBluetooth may
        drop silently.

  * src/platform/apple/l2cap.rs
      - The reactor thread no longer polls its run loop every 50 ms. Commands
        signal a `CFRunLoopSource` attached to the run loop, which also stops
        an idle reactor from spinning when no stream is scheduled.
      - Writes check `hasSpaceAvailable` before `write:maxLength:`, keep bytes
        the stream cannot take yet in a per-channel queue, and no longer close
        the channel when a write returns 0. The Rust-side bridge stops reading
        from the app while 64 KiB are queued (back-pressure).

2026-10-31 — Android L2CAP writes off the async runtime:

  * src/platform/android/l2cap_state.rs
      - The Rust->Kotlin bridge no longer calls `writeL2cap` from a tokio task.
        `BluetoothSocket`'s output stream blocks until the peer grants L2CAP
        credits, so a peer that stopped reading froze the whole runtime the
        task ran on for 30+ s. Each channel now has a writer thread fed by a
        bounded queue (4 chunks); the bridge task awaits queue space, which
        keeps back-pressure, and the writer thread closes the socket when the
        queue ends.

2026-10-31 — Apple connects withdrawn when their future is dropped:

  * src/platform/apple/central.rs
      - `connect` keeps its pending request in a guard. If the future is dropped before
        CoreBluetooth answers (a caller's own timeout), the guard removes the request and
        cancels the connection. Upstream left the request behind, so every later connect to
        that peripheral failed with `ConnectInFlight`.

2026-11-02 — Android L2CAP close reaches the channel:

  * src/platform/android/l2cap_state.rs
      - When Kotlin reports a socket closed, the Kotlin->Rust bridge task now
        shuts down its write half, so the app's `L2capChannel` reads EOF.
        Before, dropping that split half closed nothing while the other half
        lived, and `writeL2cap` on a forgotten socket returns without error,
        so a closed channel read as open forever.

2026-11-02 — Rebased onto upstream 0.5.1-beta.8:

  Upstream fixed most of what the 2026-10-31 entries above changed, in its own
  way: notifications queue behind `peripheralManagerIsReady` (#54 and the
  peripheral queue), writes without response wait for
  `canSendWriteWithoutResponse` (#54), the Apple L2CAP reactor sleeps on a
  `CFRunLoopSource` and queues behind `hasSpaceAvailable` (#55), and Android
  L2CAP writes run on the blocking pool rather than the runtime. Those files
  are upstream's now. What remains of ours:

  * Cargo.toml — `publish = false`; vendoring header.
  * build.rs — the Android Kotlin half is found in the Expo module.
  * src/platform/apple/central.rs — the `PendingConnect` guard on `connect`
    (a dropped connect future withdraws its request; upstream still leaves it,
    and every later connect fails with `ConnectInFlight`).
  * src/platform/android/l2cap_state.rs — a socket Kotlin reports closed reads
    as EOF on the app's channel (shutdown of the inbound bridge's write half).
